# holoscan_mcu_lite.resc -- Renode platform + firmware load for the MCU smoke
# test.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   A minimal Cortex-M4 machine with a USART, flash, internal SRAM and an
#   external SDRAM window, plus the LoadELF of the demo image. It exists to give
#   the demo's UART output somewhere to go and to give its 2 MiB of reassembly
#   heap somewhere to live. It is not a model of any specific silicon part and
#   it deliberately contains NO network peripheral: this smoke test sends and
#   receives nothing, so a plausible-looking Ethernet MAC here would be an
#   invitation to claim coverage that does not exist.
#
# MEMORY MAP (kept in sync with platform/gcc_arm_hml_mcu.ld)
#   0x08000000  2 MiB  FLASH    code, rodata, .data initialiser image
#   0x20000000  512 KiB SRAM     stack, .data, .bss
#   0x60000000  4 MiB  SDRAM    the newlib heap -- see the note below
#   0x40004400          USART2   the only peripheral
#
# WHY THERE IS AN SDRAM WINDOW
#   hml_reasm_open(&r, 0) uses the library default frame size of 1 MiB and
#   mallocs TWO 1 MiB buffers, so the image needs 2 MiB of heap. No small
#   Cortex-M has that in internal SRAM, so the heap goes in an external-SDRAM
#   window at 0x60000000, which is what a part with SDRAM behind an FMC maps
#   there. On real silicon that window needs an FMC configuration, a refresh
#   timer, pin muxing and a cache/MPU policy before the first allocation;
#   none of that exists here, because the emulated platform has no SDRAM
#   controller to initialise. A part without SDRAM must be built with
#   --frame-bytes, which passes a smaller cap to hml_reasm_open() and changes
#   nothing about which code paths run.
#
# STATUS -- READ BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE
#   This platform description, like the robot test that drives it, HAS NEVER
#   BEEN EXECUTED. The machine this harness was written on has no Renode, no
#   mono, no dotnet and no arm-none-eabi-gcc, so nothing here has been run
#   against a real Renode. That is exactly why the CTest test that uses it
#   exits 77 (skipped) rather than passing when the tooling is missing.
#   Where the peripheral model name or a register offset might differ between
#   Renode versions, the failure mode is a LOUD one: run_smoke.sh returns the
#   emulator's non-zero exit, so the test FAILS. It can never report a pass
#   that was not observed. If you have Renode and the platform does not load,
#   fix it here and say so in docs/renode.md -- do not paper over it.

:name: holoscan_mcu_lite
:description: holoscan_mcu_lite MCU smoke platform (Cortex-M4, USART, 2 MiB flash, 512 KiB SRAM, 4 MiB SDRAM window). Emulator only; not silicon.

$elf?=hml_mcu_demo.elf

mach create "hml_mcu"
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
cpu: CPU.CortexM @ sysbus
    cpuType: "cortex-m4"
    nvic: nvic

nvic: IRQControllers.NVIC @ sysbus 0xE000E000
    -> cpu@0

flash: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x08000000
    size: 0x200000

sram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x20000000
    size: 0x80000

sdram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x60000000
    size: 0x400000

uart: UART.STM32_UART @ sysbus <0x40004400, +0x100>
    -> nvic@37
"""

sysbus LoadELF $elf
# The Cortex-M takes its initial SP and reset vector from the first two words of
# the region LoadELF placed the image in, so the vector table offset must be the
# image's load address, spelled out literally: a monitor variable that happens to
# hold the text "0x08000000" is not guaranteed to be parsed as a number here, and
# a wrong value would be a silent vector fetch from 0.
cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000

# No showAnalyzer: under renode-test --console the UART is captured by the robot
# file's Create Terminal Tester, and an interactive analyzer window would only
# add a way for the run to fail for a non-code reason.
